1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:03,600
One hundred twenty-four trillion
dollars is expected to pass

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:07,732
down in America by twenty
forty-eight, Cerulli estimates.

3
00:00:08,112 --> 00:00:10,077
But eighteen trillion goes to charity.

4
00:00:10,457 --> 00:00:12,711
Fifty-four trillion
stops at a spouse first.

5
00:00:13,091 --> 00:00:15,778
So let's count what reaches
the kids in one family.

6
00:00:16,508 --> 00:00:19,683
People often call it the
biggest inheritance in history.

7
00:00:20,063 --> 00:00:23,739
But that's one number for a whole
country, stretched over decades.

8
00:00:24,119 --> 00:00:25,945
Maybe you'll inherit something one day.

9
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:28,969
Maybe you're buying a home
another family is leaving.

10
00:00:29,349 --> 00:00:30,717
Either way, this reaches you.

11
00:00:31,097 --> 00:00:33,960
So on this trip, we'll find
out why heirs might get

12
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:36,641
less than the headline
sounds, and get it later.

13
00:00:37,021 --> 00:00:38,473
And which leak is the biggest.

14
00:00:39,203 --> 00:00:41,111
I'm Leo, a cartoon moving-crew boss.

15
00:00:41,491 --> 00:00:44,932
I load one generation's boxes
and drive them to the next.

16
00:00:45,312 --> 00:00:48,558
Before I start the engine,
here's what this road has taught me.

17
00:00:48,938 --> 00:00:50,600
An inheritance isn't a number.

18
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:53,479
It's whatever is left at
the end of a long road.

19
00:00:54,209 --> 00:00:56,673
First, let's read the
label on that big number.

20
00:00:57,053 --> 00:00:58,570
It's not cash sitting in a vault.

21
00:00:58,950 --> 00:01:02,700
It's a forecast from a research
firm called Cerulli Associates,

22
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:04,886
made in late twenty twenty-four.

23
00:01:05,266 --> 00:01:10,178
Nearly one hundred trillion of it comes
from baby boomers and older generations.

24
00:01:10,558 --> 00:01:14,054
About one hundred five trillion
is expected to reach heirs.

25
00:01:14,434 --> 00:01:17,424
About eighteen trillion is
expected to go to charity.

26
00:01:17,804 --> 00:01:18,865
And here's the twist.

27
00:01:19,245 --> 00:01:22,980
The fifty-four trillion that stops
at a spouse isn't extra money,

28
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:24,372
and it isn't lost.

29
00:01:24,752 --> 00:01:25,845
It's a stop on the way.

30
00:01:26,225 --> 00:01:29,380
The money goes to the surviving
husband or wife first,

31
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:32,629
and later moves on,
to the kids or to charity.

32
00:01:33,009 --> 00:01:34,062
So it's not a leak.

33
00:01:34,442 --> 00:01:35,335
It's a detour.

34
00:01:35,715 --> 00:01:36,762
Remember that word.

35
00:01:37,492 --> 00:01:41,996
But one number for a whole country
hides what happens inside one home.

36
00:01:42,376 --> 00:01:45,546
To see the real leaks, we need
one family, and one truck.

37
00:01:46,276 --> 00:01:47,382
Meet the Okafors.

38
00:01:47,762 --> 00:01:50,167
They're an example family,
made up for this trip.

39
00:01:50,547 --> 00:01:52,160
Two parents, two grown kids,

40
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:55,067
and everything they own,
packed into ten boxes.

41
00:01:55,447 --> 00:01:56,741
Four boxes for the house.

42
00:01:57,121 --> 00:01:58,615
Four for retirement savings.

43
00:01:58,995 --> 00:02:00,158
Two for money in the bank.

44
00:02:00,538 --> 00:02:02,883
Each box is one tenth
of the family's wealth.

45
00:02:03,263 --> 00:02:05,190
There are no dollar signs, on purpose.

46
00:02:05,570 --> 00:02:07,744
This is an illustration, not an average.

47
00:02:08,124 --> 00:02:10,678
On the side of every box,
I write the same address.

48
00:02:11,058 --> 00:02:11,858
To: the kids.

49
00:02:12,232 --> 00:02:14,627
Moving rule number one: label everything.

50
00:02:15,007 --> 00:02:18,303
Moving rule number two:
check the bottom of every box.

51
00:02:18,683 --> 00:02:19,949
We'll see how that goes.

52
00:02:20,679 --> 00:02:23,368
My clipboard has six stops
between here and there.

53
00:02:23,748 --> 00:02:25,294
Some stops take boxes.

54
00:02:25,674 --> 00:02:27,900
One stop takes none, but it costs years.

55
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:30,480
And the stop that sounds
the scariest turns

56
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:33,379
out to be one most
trucks drive right past.

57
00:02:33,759 --> 00:02:34,559
Let's roll.

58
00:02:35,237 --> 00:02:36,498
Stop one is good news.

59
00:02:36,878 --> 00:02:37,934
Living a long time.

60
00:02:38,314 --> 00:02:41,080
In America,
a sixty-five-year-old can expect,

61
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:43,947
on average, about twenty
more years of life.

62
00:02:44,327 --> 00:02:46,640
That's a lot of groceries, heating bills,

63
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:49,271
doctor visits, and trips
to see the grandkids.

64
00:02:49,651 --> 00:02:52,485
And that's exactly what
retirement savings are for.

65
00:02:52,865 --> 00:02:54,093
They were built to be spent.

66
00:02:54,473 --> 00:02:56,016
So I cross out two boxes.

67
00:02:56,396 --> 00:02:57,690
Label: spent on living.

68
00:02:58,070 --> 00:02:58,929
Is that a leak?

69
00:02:59,409 --> 00:03:00,209
Not really.

70
00:03:00,576 --> 00:03:02,303
That's the boxes doing their job.

71
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:04,666
But it shows something a big total can't.

72
00:03:05,046 --> 00:03:07,369
A headline is one total
for a whole country.

73
00:03:07,749 --> 00:03:09,803
A family feels it one box at a time.

74
00:03:10,183 --> 00:03:13,443
And honestly, a long life is
the best kind of leak there is.

75
00:03:14,173 --> 00:03:16,941
Stop two is the one families
talk about the least.

76
00:03:17,321 --> 00:03:19,054
And it's the reason I carry this tape.

77
00:03:19,784 --> 00:03:20,868
Stop two is care.

78
00:03:21,248 --> 00:03:24,166
As people get older, many
need help with daily life.

79
00:03:24,546 --> 00:03:25,381
Getting dressed.

80
00:03:25,761 --> 00:03:26,561
Cooking.

81
00:03:26,753 --> 00:03:28,096
Taking medicine on time.

82
00:03:28,476 --> 00:03:32,260
U.S. government researchers estimate
that more than half of Americans

83
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:37,787
turning sixty-five will, at some
point, need serious long-term care.

84
00:03:38,167 --> 00:03:41,139
About one in five will need
it for more than five years.

85
00:03:41,519 --> 00:03:44,091
Sometimes that help comes
from family, at home.

86
00:03:44,471 --> 00:03:46,168
Sometimes it means a nursing home.

87
00:03:46,548 --> 00:03:47,715
And that bill is big.

88
00:03:48,095 --> 00:03:50,520
In the U.S., a private room in a nursing

89
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:53,364
home has a median cost
of about one hundred

90
00:03:53,364 --> 00:03:58,356
thirty thousand dollars a year, says a
twenty twenty-five survey by CareScout.

91
00:03:59,086 --> 00:04:00,519
Why does this one leak so much?

92
00:04:00,999 --> 00:04:04,400
Because Medicare, the health
insurance for older Americans,

93
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:08,222
generally doesn't pay for
long-term custodial care.

94
00:04:08,602 --> 00:04:11,693
That means help with daily
life, not hospital treatment.

95
00:04:12,073 --> 00:04:16,560
And if Medicaid, the program for
people with low income, ends up paying,

96
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:21,179
states are required to try to recover
those costs from the estate later.

97
00:04:21,559 --> 00:04:24,060
The same government
researchers estimate that

98
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:26,860
about one in seven
people turning sixty-five

99
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:29,920
could pay one hundred
thousand dollars or more

100
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:32,376
out of their own pocket
for this kind of care.

101
00:04:32,756 --> 00:04:35,007
For the Okafors, I cross out one box.

102
00:04:35,387 --> 00:04:36,215
Label: care.

103
00:04:36,595 --> 00:04:39,245
In a different family,
it could be zero boxes.

104
00:04:39,625 --> 00:04:40,578
Or it could be four.

105
00:04:41,308 --> 00:04:43,083
And this is where my job gets messy.

106
00:04:43,463 --> 00:04:45,125
The care box has a soft bottom.

107
00:04:45,505 --> 00:04:46,305
I tape it.

108
00:04:46,654 --> 00:04:48,526
Then a different corner starts to drip.

109
00:04:48,906 --> 00:04:50,184
So I tape that one too.

110
00:04:50,564 --> 00:04:51,364
Okay.

111
00:04:51,541 --> 00:04:52,748
That's a two-tape stop.

112
00:04:53,128 --> 00:04:55,100
Two stops down, three boxes gone.

113
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:56,547
And notice something.

114
00:04:56,927 --> 00:04:58,274
Nobody did anything wrong.

115
00:04:59,004 --> 00:05:01,780
Stop three looks like the
biggest leak on the whole list.

116
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:03,306
It isn't a leak at all.

117
00:05:03,686 --> 00:05:06,660
In many families, when the
first parent passes away,

118
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:08,970
the truck doesn't go straight to the kids.

119
00:05:09,350 --> 00:05:14,045
It turns around and parks across the
street, at the surviving husband or wife.

120
00:05:14,425 --> 00:05:17,902
Cerulli puts this stop at about
fifty-four trillion dollars.

121
00:05:18,282 --> 00:05:21,540
Nearly forty trillion of
that is expected to go to

122
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:24,767
widowed women from the
boomer and older generations.

123
00:05:25,147 --> 00:05:28,273
In our example, the kids'
names are still on the boxes.

124
00:05:28,653 --> 00:05:30,960
But now the boxes live with one parent,

125
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:34,129
and they have to last for the
rest of that parent's life.

126
00:05:34,859 --> 00:05:36,101
Now look at my clipboard.

127
00:05:36,481 --> 00:05:38,799
How many boxes did we lose at stop three?

128
00:05:39,279 --> 00:05:40,079
Zero.

129
00:05:40,204 --> 00:05:41,058
Nothing leaked.

130
00:05:41,438 --> 00:05:42,538
The kids just wait.

131
00:05:42,918 --> 00:05:44,715
But here's the part the headline hides.

132
00:05:45,095 --> 00:05:49,024
While the boxes sit in that one home,
the first two stops keep working.

133
00:05:49,404 --> 00:05:50,460
More years of living.

134
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:52,290
Maybe another round of care.

135
00:05:52,670 --> 00:05:57,062
To be fair, boxes can also grow while
they wait, if the money is invested.

136
00:05:57,442 --> 00:05:59,880
But a box that is being
spent can't grow much.

137
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:02,173
The detour doesn't take any boxes.

138
00:06:02,553 --> 00:06:04,262
It gives the other leaks more time.

139
00:06:04,642 --> 00:06:06,111
Think of it like a relay race.

140
00:06:06,491 --> 00:06:08,467
The baton goes to the partner first.

141
00:06:08,847 --> 00:06:12,519
The kids are the last runners,
waiting at the far end of the track.

142
00:06:13,249 --> 00:06:17,189
Stop four is the one part of the
headline that skips heirs altogether.

143
00:06:17,569 --> 00:06:19,016
And it happens on purpose.

144
00:06:19,746 --> 00:06:20,958
Stop four is giving.

145
00:06:21,338 --> 00:06:25,173
Cerulli expects about eighteen
trillion dollars to go to charity.

146
00:06:25,553 --> 00:06:26,353
Schools.

147
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:27,415
Hospitals.

148
00:06:27,795 --> 00:06:29,371
Churches, mosques and temples.

149
00:06:29,751 --> 00:06:30,551
Research.

150
00:06:30,833 --> 00:06:33,136
The causes people cared
about all their lives.

151
00:06:33,516 --> 00:06:37,519
This one is easy to talk about,
because it's a choice, not an accident.

152
00:06:37,899 --> 00:06:41,794
In our example, the Okafors give one
box to the school where they met.

153
00:06:42,174 --> 00:06:43,534
I cross it out with a smile.

154
00:06:43,914 --> 00:06:44,807
Label: given.

155
00:06:45,187 --> 00:06:47,590
It's still a box that won't
ride in the kids' truck.

156
00:06:47,970 --> 00:06:49,837
But nobody at the school calls it a leak.

157
00:06:50,567 --> 00:06:51,957
And this stop may change shape.

158
00:06:52,337 --> 00:06:57,480
In a Bank of America survey of wealthy
Americans, people with millions to invest,

159
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:00,380
forty-seven percent
said the next generation

160
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:02,737
is ready to take on the family's giving.

161
00:07:03,117 --> 00:07:05,681
In twenty twenty-four,
it was fifty-five percent.

162
00:07:06,061 --> 00:07:08,831
Fewer parents are sure who
will carry that box next.

163
00:07:09,561 --> 00:07:10,842
Let's check the clipboard.

164
00:07:11,222 --> 00:07:12,272
Living, minus two.

165
00:07:12,652 --> 00:07:13,574
Care, minus one.

166
00:07:13,954 --> 00:07:15,624
The detour, zero, but more time.

167
00:07:16,004 --> 00:07:16,992
Giving, minus one.

168
00:07:17,372 --> 00:07:20,650
Six boxes still on board,
and the heaviest one is next.

169
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:22,562
Stop five is the house.

170
00:07:22,942 --> 00:07:25,543
For the Okafors, it's
four boxes out of ten.

171
00:07:25,923 --> 00:07:28,288
And across the country,
it's a big box too.

172
00:07:28,668 --> 00:07:32,300
Fed data shows baby boomers
own about two-fifths of

173
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:35,361
all the real estate held
by American households.

174
00:07:35,741 --> 00:07:38,676
But a house has a problem
the other boxes don't have.

175
00:07:39,056 --> 00:07:40,262
You can't cut it in half.

176
00:07:40,642 --> 00:07:41,786
Two kids, one house.

177
00:07:42,166 --> 00:07:43,285
One wants to keep it.

178
00:07:43,665 --> 00:07:44,853
The other needs the cash.

179
00:07:45,233 --> 00:07:48,513
If neither can buy the other out,
the house goes up for sale.

180
00:07:48,893 --> 00:07:50,480
And selling has its own costs.

181
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:51,704
Agent fees.

182
00:07:52,084 --> 00:07:53,830
Repairs that were put off for years.

183
00:07:54,210 --> 00:07:56,828
Property taxes and bills
while it sits empty.

184
00:07:57,208 --> 00:08:00,867
Sometimes the house was sold years
earlier, to pay for stop two.

185
00:08:01,597 --> 00:08:03,102
None of this takes a whole box.

186
00:08:03,482 --> 00:08:04,431
It takes a slice.

187
00:08:04,811 --> 00:08:08,918
And every house sold on this road
is a house someone else gets to buy.

188
00:08:09,298 --> 00:08:10,098
Maybe you.

189
00:08:10,350 --> 00:08:12,150
Oh, and there's my third drippy corner.

190
00:08:12,530 --> 00:08:13,330
More tape.

191
00:08:13,962 --> 00:08:16,252
Stop six is the one that
sounds the scariest.

192
00:08:16,632 --> 00:08:17,432
Taxes.

193
00:08:17,681 --> 00:08:19,326
So let's read the sign on this stop.

194
00:08:19,706 --> 00:08:23,280
As of twenty twenty-six,
the U.S. federal estate tax

195
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:26,599
only starts above fifteen
million dollars per person.

196
00:08:26,979 --> 00:08:29,077
Now compare that with a typical family.

197
00:08:29,457 --> 00:08:33,040
For families headed by someone
sixty-five to seventy-four,

198
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:35,936
the median net worth was
about four hundred ten

199
00:08:35,936 --> 00:08:39,465
thousand dollars in twenty
twenty-two, the Fed found.

200
00:08:39,845 --> 00:08:42,443
The tax line is dozens of
times higher than that.

201
00:08:42,823 --> 00:08:46,895
So for most families, the federal
estate tax never comes into play.

202
00:08:47,275 --> 00:08:49,477
The Okafors drive right past this stop.

203
00:08:49,857 --> 00:08:51,245
But that's the estate tax.

204
00:08:51,625 --> 00:08:52,876
It's not the only tax.

205
00:08:53,256 --> 00:08:56,220
Money left in some
retirement accounts can still

206
00:08:56,220 --> 00:08:58,927
be taxed as income when
the kids take it out.

207
00:08:59,307 --> 00:09:03,781
Some states have their own estate or
inheritance taxes, with their own rules.

208
00:09:04,161 --> 00:09:07,036
And other countries draw the
line in different places.

209
00:09:07,416 --> 00:09:09,760
In the U.K., as of twenty twenty-six,

210
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:15,124
inheritance tax generally starts above
three hundred twenty-five thousand pounds.

211
00:09:15,854 --> 00:09:17,609
That's the surprise of this whole road.

212
00:09:17,989 --> 00:09:22,892
The tax that sounds the scariest, the
estate tax, is one most trucks never hit.

213
00:09:23,272 --> 00:09:26,390
For most families, the leaks
that matter are the quiet ones.

214
00:09:26,770 --> 00:09:27,570
Living.

215
00:09:27,718 --> 00:09:28,518
Care.

216
00:09:28,569 --> 00:09:29,369
And time.

217
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:31,443
Quick question before the last stop.

218
00:09:31,823 --> 00:09:34,742
Which leak did you think was
the biggest before this trip?

219
00:09:35,222 --> 00:09:36,271
Tell me in the comments.

220
00:09:36,651 --> 00:09:39,003
I'm curious how many people picked taxes.

221
00:09:39,733 --> 00:09:42,016
Which brings us to the
last sign on stop six.

222
00:09:42,396 --> 00:09:43,386
It isn't a cost.

223
00:09:43,766 --> 00:09:44,588
It's a clock.

224
00:09:45,318 --> 00:09:49,291
Put the long life and the detour
together, and the boxes arrive late.

225
00:09:49,671 --> 00:09:52,840
The kids on the label might have
grey hair of their own by then.

226
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:55,117
You can see it in Cerulli's numbers.

227
00:09:55,497 --> 00:09:59,320
In the first ten years, Gen X
is expected to inherit the most,

228
00:09:59,660 --> 00:10:01,796
about fourteen trillion dollars.

229
00:10:02,176 --> 00:10:04,420
But over the whole stretch
to twenty forty-eight,

230
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:07,780
millennials are expected to
end up with the biggest share,

231
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:09,574
about forty-six trillion.

232
00:10:09,954 --> 00:10:14,325
A lot of this money reaches people in the
middle of their lives, not at the start.

233
00:10:14,705 --> 00:10:16,779
And waiting has a quiet cost of its own.

234
00:10:17,159 --> 00:10:20,000
Even at the Fed's two
percent inflation target,

235
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:24,460
a dollar loses about a third of
its buying power over twenty years.

236
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:28,340
Money that arrives when you're starting
out might help with a first home,

237
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:29,889
or a first business.

238
00:10:30,269 --> 00:10:33,020
Money that arrives later
often goes somewhere else.

239
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:34,452
Your own retirement.

240
00:10:34,832 --> 00:10:35,707
Your own kids.

241
00:10:36,087 --> 00:10:37,021
Same boxes.

242
00:10:37,401 --> 00:10:38,233
Different job.

243
00:10:38,963 --> 00:10:42,055
So, how many boxes are left
when the doors finally open?

244
00:10:42,785 --> 00:10:43,691
The truck stops.

245
00:10:44,071 --> 00:10:46,899
I open the doors, and I
read my clipboard out loud.

246
00:10:47,279 --> 00:10:48,532
Loaded: ten boxes.

247
00:10:48,912 --> 00:10:50,514
Stop one, living: minus two.

248
00:10:50,894 --> 00:10:52,388
Stop two, care: minus one.

249
00:10:52,768 --> 00:10:55,922
Stop three, the detour:
minus zero, plus a few years.

250
00:10:56,302 --> 00:10:57,845
Stop four, giving: minus one.

251
00:10:58,225 --> 00:11:00,191
Stop five, the house: minus a slice.

252
00:11:00,571 --> 00:11:04,005
Stop six, the estate tax:
minus zero, for this family.

253
00:11:04,385 --> 00:11:07,283
Income tax on the
retirement boxes: a sliver.

254
00:11:07,663 --> 00:11:12,460
Delivered: six boxes, minus a slice
and a sliver, years later than planned.

255
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:16,876
Split between two kids, that's about
three boxes each, a little less.

256
00:11:17,606 --> 00:11:18,776
Is that a sad story?

257
00:11:19,256 --> 00:11:20,362
Look at the list again.

258
00:11:20,742 --> 00:11:21,542
A long life.

259
00:11:21,885 --> 00:11:22,974
Care when it was needed.

260
00:11:23,354 --> 00:11:24,393
A gift they chose.

261
00:11:24,773 --> 00:11:26,870
That's not a family that lost its money.

262
00:11:27,250 --> 00:11:28,698
It's a family that used it.

263
00:11:29,078 --> 00:11:30,963
And remember, this is one example.

264
00:11:31,343 --> 00:11:35,126
A family with smaller savings
might arrive with no boxes at all.

265
00:11:35,506 --> 00:11:38,920
A very wealthy family might
pay a real bill at stop six,

266
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:41,139
and still arrive with a full truck.

267
00:11:41,519 --> 00:11:43,763
The point of the list
isn't to judge anyone.

268
00:11:44,143 --> 00:11:46,217
It's to see where the boxes actually went.

269
00:11:46,947 --> 00:11:49,082
Now zoom out, back to that giant number.

270
00:11:49,462 --> 00:11:53,650
Cerulli expects about half of it,
around sixty-two trillion dollars,

271
00:11:54,020 --> 00:11:56,496
to come from just two
percent of households.

272
00:11:56,876 --> 00:11:57,964
The wealthiest ones.

273
00:11:58,344 --> 00:12:02,477
And Fed data tells a similar story
about the wealth households hold.

274
00:12:02,857 --> 00:12:05,271
The top ten percent hold
about two-thirds of it.

275
00:12:05,651 --> 00:12:07,668
The bottom half holds about two percent.

276
00:12:08,048 --> 00:12:09,303
So the headline is real.

277
00:12:09,683 --> 00:12:10,701
It's just lumpy.

278
00:12:11,081 --> 00:12:13,578
For a few families, the
truck is a whole convoy.

279
00:12:13,958 --> 00:12:16,404
For most, it's a few boxes,
and they come late.

280
00:12:16,784 --> 00:12:20,234
That's why two people can read the
same headline and both be right.

281
00:12:20,614 --> 00:12:21,750
One sees a convoy.

282
00:12:22,130 --> 00:12:23,678
The other sees a few boxes.

283
00:12:24,408 --> 00:12:25,552
Could this road change?

284
00:12:26,032 --> 00:12:26,832
It could.

285
00:12:27,051 --> 00:12:30,250
If care keeps getting more
expensive, stop two may grow.

286
00:12:30,630 --> 00:12:33,008
If tax laws change, stop six may move.

287
00:12:33,388 --> 00:12:36,832
This isn't a forecast for any
family, and it isn't a plan.

288
00:12:37,212 --> 00:12:39,247
It's a map of where the boxes tend to go.

289
00:12:39,977 --> 00:12:43,870
So the next time someone says one
hundred twenty-four trillion dollars,

290
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:45,264
picture the truck.

291
00:12:45,644 --> 00:12:47,306
An inheritance isn't a number.

292
00:12:47,686 --> 00:12:50,185
It's whatever is left at
the end of a long road.

293
00:12:50,565 --> 00:12:52,899
And on this road, I'm
going to need more tape.

294
00:12:53,629 --> 00:12:55,966
There's one box we only
opened for a minute.

295
00:12:56,346 --> 00:12:57,146
The house.

296
00:12:57,342 --> 00:13:01,338
So what happens to a whole street
when many houses change hands at once?
